Meeting Summary
1. Repository Unification & CI/CD Pipeline
Jacob proposed merging the middleware and the front-end UI into a single repository to ensure synchronized version tracking and seamless integration. This change simplifies development workflows and allows the creation of a single CI/CD pipeline that builds both components into a single Docker image. Sagar and Rishabh approved the move, noting it will eliminate instances where local developer environments miss updates due to untracked branch pulls.

2. UI Graph Reliability & Gaps Handling
To solve the issue of data gaps occurring during server downtime, Jacob suggested changing the rendering scheme. Instead of dynamically appending incoming data points, the UI will pre-allocate a circular data buffer representing a fixed 12-hour window (sampling every 5 seconds) initialized entirely with NaN (null) values. Real-time and historical entries will then be mapped strictly into specific indices based on their absolute timestamps. Sagar expressed performance concerns regarding continuous array looping over 12 hours of data points and potential conflicts with SciChart's native caching/FIFO mechanism. However, he noted that the tile view already uses custom SVGs, making the implementation easier there.

3. API Deconstruction & Performance Optimization
The current backend features heavily clustered endpoints (e.g., the "beds" API) that fetch layout bounds, current parameters, limits, and historical data all in a single block, creating heavy database queries with complex joins. This blocks page loading and forces multiple loading animations ("spinners"). The team agreed to break these down into smaller, progressive frontend queries:

High Priority: Grid layout and bed status (loads instantly).

Medium Priority: Real-time values, configurations, and navigation details.

Low Priority: Heavy historical chart data fetched asynchronously.

4. Database Schema Cleanup
Jacob highlighted an anti-pattern in the current architecture where relational database tables store complex JSON strings as plain text blobs. This prevents native indexing, filtering, and sorting. The team agreed to map out these unstructured fields and convert them into properly typed columns or related tables.

